{"reference":"2 Samuel 13","book":"2 Samuel","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"The chapter’s moral focus","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter does not treat Amnon’s act as a romance or a merely private family conflict. Tamar calls his intended act folly and warns of its shame; afterward she is left desolate, while Absalom’s hatred and later revenge grow from what Amnon did. The account therefore explicitly condemns Amnon’s violation and keeps Tamar’s suffering in view rather than reducing her to a figure in the royal family’s struggle for succession.","verse_refs":["2 Samuel 13:1-39"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_013_e7ea4930e12a0ef2f86d98d6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within 2 Samuel","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Second Samuel’s wider story moves from David’s rise and the consolidation of his kingdom, through the ark and the promise of a dynasty, into royal abuse, violence within David’s household, rebellion, and grief. This chapter belongs to that darker movement within the book.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_samuel_013_c00da03bacbd10ae43830e27"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}